Three years ago in europe only weak 40bit SSL-encryption was possible. So we decided to use a java applet with built in 128bit encryption from Brokat Infosystems to get reasonable security. This java applet uses a}{\lang1033\langfe1031\langnp1033 n}{
\lang1033\langfe1031\langnp1033 
 encryption algorithm called SRT, which  is similar to SSL but uses IDEA as symmetric encryption. The applet itself is 130KB in size which is not signed, so everybody have has to download it from the server. We did not sign the applet because we don
\rquote t want to install it on the client, and get all the risks of weak client configurations. On server side a CGI catches up the encrypted data and forwards it to a middleware which handles the database connectivity.
